// Stationary iterative solver (issue #53): Jacobi for A x = b, K fixed sweeps.
//
// The Jacobi iteration in RESIDUAL form (so the diagonal need not be excluded from the
// sum) is  x^{k} = x^{k-1} + D^{-1} ( b - A x^{k-1} ),  i.e. per row
//   x^{k}_i = x^{k-1}_i + ( b_i - sum_j A(i,j) x^{k-1}_j ) / A(i,i).
// One sweep is a gemv (the reduction over j) plus an axpy-style update; the iteration
// index k is a third axis, and each sweep reads ONLY the previous sweep's x -- Jacobi is
// FULLY PARALLEL within a sweep. (Gauss-Seidel would instead read x^{k}_j for j < i, an
// intra-sweep triangular wavefront that serializes the sweep; see stationary.md.)
//
// Index space (i,j,k): i = row / unknown (0..N-1), j = column / reduction (0..N-1),
// k = iteration (0..K-1). Reading x^{k-1}_j across every row is the matrix-vector
// GATHER: the taps xs(i,N-1,k-1) and xs(j,N-1,k-1) project onto the finished-solution
// face j = N-1, so they are AFFINE -- Jacobi is a SARE (the honest broadcast of the
// whole previous iterate), though a benign FORWARD one (each sweep reads only sweep k-1).
//
// SCHEDULE -- free only. Like the triangular solve, each row's gemv reduction and its
// residual divide FUSE at the finishing cell (i, N-1, k): xs there reads the completed
// acc(i,N-1,k) at the same lattice point, a zero-slack self-dependence no linear tau can
// order. The data-flow-earliest FREE schedule runs it: within a sweep every row solves
// in parallel (Jacobi's parallelism is the wide wavefront); across sweeps the k-1
// dependence serializes -- exactly the structure the schedule should show.
//
// Run it:   dfactl --sure docs/SURE/stationary.sure            (free schedule)

N = 3; K = 8;

system ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, 0 <= j < N, 0 <= k < K) {
    am(i,j,k)   = am(i,j,k-1);                                             // A(i,j), held across sweeps (fed k=-1)
    bb(i,j,k)   = bb(i,j-1,k);                                             // b(i), propagated along +j (fed j=-1)
    diag(i,j,k) = diag(i,j-1,k);                                          // A(i,i), propagated along +j (read locally)
    acc(i,j,k)  = acc(i,j-1,k) + am(i,j,k) * xs(j,N-1,k-1);               // reduce sum_j A(i,j) x^{k-1}_j (affine tap x_j)
    xs(i,j,k)   = xs(i,N-1,k-1) + (bb(i,j,k) - acc(i,j,k)) / diag(i,j,k);  // residual update; correct at j = N-1
}

// tensor confluences
input  A[N][N] ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, 0 <= j < N, k = -1)      : am(i,j,k)   = A[i][j];  // A held from k = -1
input  B[N]    ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, j = -1, 0 <= k < K)      : bb(i,j,k)   = B[i];      // b on the j = -1 face
input  Diag[N] ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, j = -1, 0 <= k < K)      : diag(i,j,k) = Diag[i];   // A(i,i) on the j = -1 face
input          ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, j = -1, 0 <= k < K)      : acc(i,j,k)  = 0;         // reduction seed
input  X0[N]   ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, 0 <= j < N, k = -1)      : xs(i,j,k)   = X0[i];      // initial guess x^{-1} = x^0
output X[N]    ((i,j,k) | 0 <= i < N, j = N-1, K-1 <= k, k < K) : X[i] = xs(i,j,k);        // x after K sweeps (k=K-1)

// no linear tau: the per-row reduction and its residual divide fuse at (i,N-1,k); the
// free schedule is the sweep wavefront (parallel within a sweep, serial across sweeps).

// test bench:  strongly diagonally dominant A, exact solution x = [1,1,1].
//   A = [[10,1,1],[1,10,1],[1,1,10]],  b = [12,12,12],  Diag = [10,10,10],  x0 = 0.
//   K = 8 Jacobi sweeps converge to x = [1,1,1].
data A    = { 10,  1,  1,
               1, 10,  1,
               1,  1, 10 };
data B    = { 12, 12, 12 };
data Diag = { 10, 10, 10 };
data X0   = {  0,  0,  0 };
